From 0985a1c0097a86c999ed2a3dbe76ae30731fc237 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Keno Fischer <keno@chroniton.ai>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 22:35:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] JIT: count rr's software ticks in the generated code

rr (https://rr-project.org) can record and replay a program without the
CPU's branch counter (in VMs, on CPUs without a usable counter) when the
program is built with a compiler pass that counts "software ticks": the
pass places a short, fixed code sequence, a tick, so that no instruction
runs twice in the same stack frame without a tick in between (at function
entries and loop heads). rr preempts threads, and places asynchronous
signals on replay, by the tick count. The pass predefines __RR_SOFTTICKS__
to the version of the ABI (1), which rr's include/rr/softticks.h defines.

The pass does not see the machine code the JIT compiler generates. While a
thread matches in JIT code, rr cannot switch to another thread, and a
signal that arrives there can only be found again on replay by stopping at
its address on every pass and comparing registers. In our tests, a thread
was not preempted for the whole of a long match, and replaying a recording
of a 0.1 second run with signals in JIT code took about two minutes.

When __RR_SOFTTICKS__ is defined, emit the tick (with sljit_emit_op_custom)
at the start of the generated function and at every label made by LABEL():
loop heads, the targets of backtracking jumps, the entries of fast-call
subroutines, and (after their ENDBR) the targets of indirect jumps. Labels
that only forward jumps reach get none: JUMPHERE() already makes such
labels, and set_jumps() of a new label now says so with FORWARD_LABEL().
The x86 tick clobbers the status flags, which sljit never keeps live across
a label; the ARM64 tick clobbers x16 and x17, which the JIT does not
allocate. Supported on x86-64, x86-32 and ARM64, the architectures rr
supports; elsewhere __RR_SOFTTICKS__ is an error.

Without __RR_SOFTTICKS__ nothing changes: the compiled library is
byte-identical (checked on x86-64 and ARM64).

Tested on x86-64 and x86-32 with RunTest, RunGrepTest and pcre2_jit_test,
also in a debug build (whose sljit argument checks reject status flags used
across a label), and by checking each code buffer the tests generate: every
backward jump, call and indirect jump target is a tick, and the code has no
cycle without one.
